\[
R_S(t) = R_1(t) \times R_2(t) \times R_3(t) \times R_3 \times R_4 \times R_5 \times R_6 \times R_7 \times R_7 \times R_8 \times R_8 \times R_9 \times R_9 \times R_{10} \times R_{10} \times R_{11} \times R_{11} \times R_{12} \times R_{12} \cdots R_N(t)
\]

This can be written as:

\[
R_S(t) = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{I=1}^{N} R_I(t)
\]

**Note**: The original answer provided \( R_S(t) = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{I=1}^{N} R_I(t) \), which is the **average mean-value distribution (AMVD)** for a series system, not the reliability function. The correct formula is the product of the individual reliability functions.

