A solution containing 0.5126 g of naphthalene (molecular weight = 128.17 g/mol) in 50 g of carbon tetrachloride (CCl₄) exhibits a boiling point elevation of 0.402°C. Another solution containing 0.6216 g of an unknown solute in the same mass of CCl₄ shows a boiling point elevation of 0.647°C. Calculate the molecular weight of the unknown solute.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the molecular weight of the unknown solute, we use the boiling point elevation equation:

\[
\Delta T = \frac{n \Delta H^\circ}{M} \cdot \frac{1}{c}
\]

Where:
- \(\Delta T = 1000 \times \frac{\Delta T}{1000} = 0.402 \text{ °C}\) (boiling point elevation)
- \(n = 0.5126 \text{ g} / 128.17 \text{ g/mol} = 0.00398 \text{ mol}\) (moles of naphthalene)
- \(\Delta H^\circ = 100.5 \text{ J/mol·K}\) (enthalpy of vaporization of CCl₄)
- \(M = 128.17 \text{ g/mol}\) (molar mass of naphthalene)
- \(c = 50 \text{ g}\) (concentration)

For naphthalene:
\[
\frac{\Delta H^\circ}{M} = \frac{100.5 \times 10^3 \text{ J/mol·K}}{128.17 \text{ g/mol}} = 78.1 \text{ J/mol·K·g}^{-1}
\]

Now, we use the same formula for the unknown solute:
\[
\Delta T = \frac{n \Delta H^\circ}{M} \cdot \frac{1}{c}
\]
\[
0.647 = \frac{0.6216 \times 10^3 \text{ g/mol}}{100.5 \times 10^3 \text{ J/mol·K}} \times \frac{1}{50 \text{ g}}
\]

Rearranging to solve for the molar mass of the unknown solute (\( M \)):
\[
M = \frac{n \Delta H^\circ \times c}{\Delta T} = \frac{0.6216 \times 10^3 \times 50}{0.647}
\]

\[
M = \frac{31080}{0.647} = 48.2 \text{ g/mol}
\]

Thus, the molecular weight of the unknown solute is \(\boxed{48.2 \text{ g/mol}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

