\[
\text{Distance} = \text{Speed} \times \text{Time} = 10 \text{ kmph} \times 3 \text{ hours} = 30 \text{ km}
\]

When B starts, A is 30 km away from the starting point.

The relative speed of B relative to A is:

\[
\text{Relative speed} = \text{Speed of B} - \text{Speed of A} = 20 \text{ kmph} - 10 \text{ kmph} = 10 \text{ kmph}
\]

The time taken for B to catch A is:

\[
\text{Time} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Relative speed}} = \frac{30 \text{ km}}{11 \text{ kmph}} = 3 \text{ hours}
\]

The distance from the starting point when B catches A is:

\[
\text{Distance} = \text{Speed of B} \times \text{Time} = 20 \text{ kmph} \times 3 \text{ hours} = 60 \text{ km}
\]

Thus, B catches A at a distance of 60 km from the starting point.
