<start_of_turn>model
","The decay length of an evanescent wave is given by:
\[
\lambda / (2n_f)
\]
where \(\lambda\) is the wavelength and \(n_f\) is the refractive index of the front (clssler) material. 

Given:
- \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \sim 0.3\).
- \(n_{\text{eff}} = 0.3 + n_f\)
- \(\lambda = 1 \, \mu m = 10^6 \, \text{nm}\)

The decay length is:
\[
\lambda / (2n_f) = 10^6 \, \text{nm} / (2n_f)
\]
To find \(n_f\), we use the given relationship \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \approx 0.3\). The refractive index of GaAs is approximately 1.51 and the refractive index of Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al\(_{text{0.8}}\) is approximately 1.12.

Assuming \(n_f = n_{\text{cllector}}\) and \(n_{\text{core}} = n_{\text{GaAs}}\), the decay length is:
\[
\lambda / (2n_f) = 10^6 \, \text{nm} / (2 \times 1.12) = 440 \, \text{nm}
\]

