u(x, y, z) & v(x, y, z) & w(x, z)
\end{vmatrix},
\]
where \(\mathbf{V} = \langle u(x, y, z), v(x, y, z), w(x, z) \rangle\). 

Given that \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) depends only on \(x\) and \(y\), we can write \(\mathbf{V} = \langle u(x, y), v(x, y), 0 \rangle\). Substituting this into the curl formula, we get:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \begin{vmatrix}
\hat{i} & \hat{j} & \hat{k} \\
\frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & \0 \\
u(x, y) & v(x, y) & 0
\end{vvmatrix}.
\]
Expanding the determinant along the third component:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left(\frac{\partial}{\partial y} \cdot 0 - 0\right) \hat{i} - \left(\frac{\partial}{\partial x} \cdot 0 - 0\right) \hat{j} + \left(\frac{\partial}{\partial x} v(x, y) - \frac{\partial}{\partial y} u(x, y)\right) \hat{k}.
\]
Simplifying, we get:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = 0 - 0 + \left(\frac{\partial v}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial u}{\partial y}\right) \hat{k}.
\]
Thus, the curl of \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) is:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left(\frac{\partial v}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial u}{\partial y}\right) \hat{k}.
\]

The curl of \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) is \(\boxed{\left(\frac{\partial v}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial u}{\partial y}\right) \hat{k}}\).
