","To solve this problem, we need to consider the concept of time dilation in special relativity. Time dilation occurs when an object is moving at a high speed relative to an other observer. The time experienced by the object (e.g., the clocks on the spaceship) will appear slower compared to the time experienced by the observer (e.g., on Earth).

The time dilation formula is:

t' = t / sqrt(1 - v^2/c^2)

where t' is the time experienced by the observer on Earth, t is the time experienced by the spaceship, and v is the relative velocity of the spaceship relative to Earth, and c is the speed of light.

In this problem, we are given that the time experienced by the clock in the cockpit is 3 years when the ship returns to Earth. We need to find the time experienced by the clock in the cabin when it is compared to a clock on Earth. Since both clocks are on the same spaceship, they will experience the same time dilation.

First, we need to find the time experienced on Earth (t') when the clock in the cockpit shows 3 years (t). We are given that the spaceship's velocity is 0.6c, so v = 0.6c.

t' = 3 years / sqrt(1 - (0.6c)^2/c^2)
t' = 3 years / sqrt(1 - 0.36)
t' = 3 years / sqrt(0.64)
t' = 3 years / 0.8
t' = 3.75 years

So, when the clock in the cockpit shows 3 years, the time experienced on Earth is 3.75 years. Since both clocks on the spaceship are in the same frame of reference, they will both experience the same time dilation. Therefore, the time spent on the clock in the cabin will also be 3 years when the ship returns to Earth.",fluency,"# Task Description
